## 14. Granularity behavior (CF vs Budget granularity)

The CF inherits the budget's granularity. **Payment terms options and their calculation rules are identical across all granularities** — the dropdown always shows Cash / 30+ / 60+ / 90+ / 120+ / 180+, and the cash-out month is always computed as `expense month + lag-in-days`, regardless of whether columns are months, quarters, or a single FY.

Rationale: payment lags do not snap to column boundaries. An expense booked in March with 60+ terms hits cash in May; if the budget is quarterly, that means the expense lives in Q1 but the cash impact lands in Q2. The system must therefore compute the cash hit at day-level and then aggregate into whichever column the budget uses.

| Budget granularity | CF column layout | Payment terms behavior |
|---|---|---|
| Monthly | 12 monthly columns + FY across every CF table. | Cash = same month; 30+ = +1 month; 60+ = +2 months; 90+ = +3 months; 120+ = +4 months; 180+ = +6 months. Cash hits that land beyond December roll into the next FY's opening AP/AR balance and are shown in a "Carry to next FY" footer row. |
| Quarterly | 4 quarterly columns + FY across every CF table. | Same dropdown. Internally the system assumes each quarterly expense bucket lands at the **midpoint of the quarter** (mid-month-2 of the quarter) and applies the lag in days. Resulting cash hit is bucketed into whichever quarter contains that date. Example: an expense in Q1 with 60+ terms → expense midpoint mid-Feb + 60 days = mid-Apr → cash hits **Q2**. With 30+ terms → mid-Mar → still **Q1**. |
| Yearly | 1 FY column. | Same dropdown. Cash hits within the FY bucket into FY. Cash hits past Dec-31 (i.e. 120+/180+ on Q3/Q4 expenses, or any term applied to a late-FY expense) roll into a **"Carry to next FY"** footer row, which becomes the opening AP/AR balance of the next CF. |

The Period toggle on the CF Forecast view (§11.1) can only **roll up** to coarser granularities, never down (i.e. a Yearly budget cannot be viewed Monthly in CF).

